# rules.topic -- the language rules a program must follow, each stated once
# with its reason, its diagnostic and its alternative (COMPILERPEDIA.md).

@topic rule.if-placement
title: Where an if can be a value
summary: An if chain is a value only as the whole right side of "=", the whole value of "return" or a statement of its own; never an operand, a call argument or a list element.
index: rules errors
alias: if as an argument; if arguments; if as an operand; inline if; one-line if; ternary operator; conditional expression inside a call
keywords: if, elif, else, value, operand, argument, concat, ternary, whole right side
related: concept.if-expressions rule.loop-placement rule.indentation rule.control-placement principle.bind-before-use principle.small-functions error.surface-syntax
docs: README.md ELIF.md METHOD-SUGAR.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | an "if" value must be the whole right side*

== THE RULE
An `if` chain (`if`, any number of `elif`, an optional `else`) evaluates
to the value of the branch that runs. It may stand only where its blocks
get lines of their own:

  * the whole right side of a binding:      sign = if n < 0: ...
  * the whole right side of a destructuring: {a, b} = if ok: ...
  * the whole value of `return`:             return if ok: ...
  * a statement of its own; as the last statement of a function body (or
    of the program) it is that body's value.

It is never an operand (`1 + if c: ...`), a call or method argument
(`f(if c: ...)`, `s.concat(if c: ...)`), a list or struct element, or a
method receiver. There is no one-line form (`if c: a else: b`) and no `?:`
operator. `break` takes no value at all (rule.control-placement), so it is
not a place for an `if` either.

== WHY
The branches of an `if` are blocks, and a block is delimited by
indentation. Inside parentheses and brackets the layout is ignored
(rule.indentation), so a block cannot be written there; the places above
are exactly those where the `if` ends the statement and its blocks can
follow on their own lines. Botlish has one conditional form, and no
second, inline one (`?:`, `a if c else b`) with a precedence of its own.
Naming the value first also names that part of the computation
(principle.bind-before-use).

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX, at the word `if`:

    an "if" value must be the whole right side of "=" or "return"

The one-line form `x = if ok: "yes" else: "no"` is reported earlier, at
the first branch: `expected a new line and an indented block after ":"`
(rule.indentation).

@example reject
import std::str

fn answer(ok: bool) -> str:
    "answer: ".concat(if ok: "yes" else: "no")

0
@caption An if as a method argument
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:23
@message an "if" value must be the whole right side of "=" or "return"
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Bind the `if` to a name, then use the name; or move the choice into a
small function whose body is the `if` (or a guard and a value), and call
it where the value is needed.

@example accept
import std::str

fn answer(ok: bool) -> str:
    word = if ok:
        "yes"
    else:
        "no"
    "answer: ".concat(word)

answer(true)
@caption Bind the if's value first
@value "answer: yes"
@end

@example accept
import std::str

fn yes_no(ok: bool) -> str:
    if ok:
        return "yes"
    "no"

fn answer(ok: bool) -> str:
    "answer: ".concat(ok.yes_no())

answer(false)
@caption Or move the choice into a helper
@value "answer: no"
@end

@topic rule.loop-placement
title: Where a collecting loop can be a value
summary: A loop with an iteration clause evaluates to the List of its body's values, but only as the whole right side of "=", the value of "return" or a statement of its own; never as an argument or operand.
index: rules errors
alias: loop arguments; loop as an argument; loop as an operand; loop inside a call; collecting loop as argument
keywords: loop, in, from, to, through, collecting, argument, operand, list of values
related: concept.loops rule.if-placement rule.indentation rule.control-placement principle.bind-before-use error.surface-syntax
docs: README.md COLLECTING-LOOPS.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected an expression, found "loop"*

== THE RULE
A loop with an iteration clause (`loop x in xs:`, `loop i from a to b:`,
`... through b:`, `loop i down from b to a:`, lockstep clauses joined by
`and`) evaluates to the List of its body's values. Like an `if`
(rule.if-placement), it is a value only where its body gets lines of its
own:

  * the whole right side of a binding:   ok = loop x in xs: ...
  * the whole value of `return`:          return loop x in xs: ...
  * a statement of its own; as the last statement of a function body (or
    of the program) it is that body's value.

It is never a call or method argument, an operand, a list element or a
receiver: `all_true?(loop b in xs: b.ok?())` and `n = 1 + loop ...` are
syntax errors. A loop's body is always a block on its own lines; there is
no one-line loop.

== WHY
The body of a loop is a block, delimited by indentation, and layout is
ignored inside parentheses and brackets (rule.indentation). A collecting
loop is therefore written where the statement ends, and the List it builds
is used by name. The rule is the same as an `if`'s, so there is one rule
for every construct that has a block.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX, at the word `loop`:

    expected an expression, found "loop"

This is the parser's general message for a token that cannot start an
expression; with "loop" it always means a loop written where a value is
expected.

@example reject
fn all_true?(flags: List[bool]) -> bool:
    loop f in flags:
        if not f:
            return false
    true

xs = [3, 5, 8]
all_true?(loop x in xs: x > 0)
@caption A loop as a call argument
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 8:11
@message expected an expression, found "loop"
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Bind the loop's List to a name, then pass the name; or make the loop the
body (or the `return` value) of a small function.

@example accept
fn all_true?(flags: List[bool]) -> bool:
    loop f in flags:
        if not f:
            return false
    true

xs = [3, 5, 8]
positive = loop x in xs:
    x > 0
positive.all_true?()
@caption Bind the collected List first
@value true
@end

@topic rule.forward-references
title: No reference to a later binding
summary: A binding is visible only from where it is established onward; a reference to a function or value defined later in the same scope is UNBOUND, even inside a function body that runs later.
index: rules errors
alias: forward reference; is declared later; use before definition; call a function defined below
keywords: forward, later, declared later, hoisting, order, callee first, mutual recursion, unbound
related: concept.definition-order concept.mutual-recursion concept.bindings rule.immutable-bindings rule.module-bindings principle.bind-before-use error.unbound
docs: STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md MUTUAL-RECURSION.md MODULE-BINDINGS.md
match: CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND | *declared later*

== THE RULE
A name becomes visible where its binding is established -- a value after
its initializer, a function at its `fn` line -- and stays visible to the
end of its scope and in the scopes nested inside it. A reference to a name
that is bound only later is an error:

  * in a function body, even when the function is only called after the
    later binding exists;
  * in a branch that never runs and in a function that is never called;
  * in a module: a module value may read earlier module values only, and a
    module function may call only functions defined above it.

Nothing is hoisted, and the order in which things run never makes a
forward reference legal. A named function may call itself: `fn f`
establishes `f` before its own body. A value is not visible in its own
initializer (`x = x + 1` with no earlier `x` is a forward reference).

Two functions that call each other cannot be written today: `mutual:`
groups are designed (MUTUAL-RECURSION.md) but not implemented, and
`mutual:` is a syntax error.

== WHY
Resolution walks the source once, in evaluation order, over scopes that
start empty: a reference's target, identity and contract are fixed by the
source before it (STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md). Every analysis then
sees a callee completely before its first caller. When forward
references were allowed, later definitions had to repair what earlier
stages could not know: the caller-before-callee order of the same
program produced about a third more specialized instances and twice the
run-time guards. "The desired fix is not a smarter hoist; it is that
later bindings do not exist."

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND, at the reference:

    unbound name "double": "double" is declared later; forward references
    are not allowed (a binding is visible only after it is established)

A name that is bound nowhere is the plain `unbound name "NAME"`
(error.unbound).

@example reject
fn total(n: int) -> int:
    double(n) + 1

fn double(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

total(3)
@caption Caller before callee
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 2:5
@message *"double" is declared later*
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Write callees before their callers: helpers first, the functions that use
them next, the top-level code last. For two functions that would call each
other, write one self-recursive function and pass what distinguishes the
two cases as a parameter, or use a loop.

@example accept
fn double(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

fn total(n: int) -> int:
    double(n) + 1

total(3)
@caption Callee first
@value 7
@end

@example accept
fn parity?(n: int, want_even: bool) -> bool:
    if n == 0:
        return want_even
    parity?(n - 1, not want_even)

parity?(10, true)
@caption even?/odd? as one recursive function
@value true
@end

@topic rule.one-handler-per-error
title: A handler names exactly one error
summary: Each "on" handler of a handled call names exactly one error; several errors take several handlers, and there is no "on A, B:", "on A | B:" or catch-all handler.
index: rules errors
alias: on A or B; one handler for two errors; handler names exactly one error; shared error handler
keywords: on, handler, handled call, several errors, comma, enum payload
related: concept.errors concept.error-payloads concept.enums rule.unhandled-errors capability.multi-error-handler task.handle-several-errors error.surface-syntax
docs: ERROR-PAYLOADS.md EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ENUMS.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | a handler names exactly one error*

== THE RULE
A handled call is a call followed by `:` and an indented block of
handlers:

    r = parse(text):
        on Empty:
            ...
        on TooLong:
            ...

Each handler names exactly one declared error, written `on NAME:`, `on
NAME details:` (the payload bound whole) or `on NAME {field, ...}:` (the
payload destructured). Several errors take several handlers. There is no
list of errors (`on A, B:`), no alternative (`on A | B:`), no handler that
catches every error, and no guard (`on A if ...:`). A second handler for
the same error is DUPLICATE. Errors the handlers do not name propagate,
and must be admitted by the enclosing function (rule.unhandled-errors).

== WHY
`on` selects by the error's identity only (ERROR-PAYLOADS.md). A handler's
payload binding has exactly the payload type of the one error it names --
the anonymous struct of that error's declared fields -- and two errors are
unrelated identities even when their fields are identical, so a handler
for two errors would have no single payload type. One name per handler
keeps selection nominal: which handler runs is visible from its name, with
no matching order or precedence to reason about.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX, at the comma:

    a handler names exactly one error: write one "on NAME:" handler per
    error

`on A | B:` is reported as `unexpected character "|"`: `|` is not a
Botlish character at all.

@example reject
error Empty
error TooLong

fn check(s: str) -> str errors Empty, TooLong:
    if s == "":
        fail Empty
    if s == "abcdef":
        fail TooLong
    s

fn safe(s: str) -> str:
    check(s):
        on Empty, TooLong:
            "invalid"

safe("ok")
@caption Two errors in one handler
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 13:17
@message a handler names exactly one error*
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Write one handler per error; when the bodies are long, call one helper
function from each. When callers usually treat the failures alike, declare
one error whose payload says which failure it was, as an enum field: a
caller that does not care writes one `on Invalid:`, and one that does
reads the field (ERROR-PAYLOADS.md, concept.enums).

@example accept
error Empty
error TooLong

fn check(s: str) -> str errors Empty, TooLong:
    if s == "":
        fail Empty
    if s == "abcdef":
        fail TooLong
    s

fn safe(s: str) -> str:
    check(s):
        on Empty:
            "invalid"
        on TooLong:
            "invalid"

[safe("ok"), safe(""), safe("abcdef")]
@caption One handler per error
@value ["ok", "invalid", "invalid"]
@end

@example accept
enum Problem:
    Empty, TooLong

error Invalid:
    problem: Problem

fn check(s: str) -> str errors Invalid:
    if s == "":
        fail Invalid {problem: Problem::Empty}
    if s == "abcdef":
        fail Invalid {problem: Problem::TooLong}
    s

fn safe(s: str) -> str:
    check(s):
        on Invalid:
            "invalid"

fn reason(s: str) -> str:
    check(s):
        on Invalid {problem}:
            if problem == Problem::Empty:
                return "empty"
            "too long"

[safe("ok"), safe(""), reason("abcdef")]
@caption One error with an enum payload
@value ["ok", "invalid", "too long"]
@end

@topic rule.unhandled-errors
title: Every possible error of a call is handled, admitted or proven impossible
summary: Each declared error a call may produce must be handled at the call, admitted by the enclosing function's errors clause, or proven impossible by the compiler; otherwise the call is UNHANDLED-ERROR.
index: rules
alias: errors must be handled; admit an error; propagate an error
keywords: errors clause, handler, on, fail, propagate, declared error, completion, IndexNotFound
related: concept.errors concept.error-payloads concept.known-errors proof.completions proof.list-bounds rule.known-error rule.one-handler-per-error principle.errors-over-sentinels task.handle-several-errors error.unhandled-error
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md

== THE RULE
A function's `errors` clause (`fn f(x) -> T errors E1, E2:`) declares the
errors a call to it may produce; a standard-library function or intrinsic
declares its own (`std::list::at` produces IndexNotFound, `argv` produces
InvalidArgumentEncoding). Every error a call may produce must be accounted
for at the call, in one of three ways:

  * handled: the call is a handled call with an `on NAME:` handler for it;
  * admitted: the enclosing function declares it in its own `errors`
    clause, and it propagates to that function's callers;
  * proven impossible: the compiler proves, from the facts it has about
    the arguments at this call, that the callee cannot produce it
    (proof.completions); such a call needs nothing.

The program's top level has no `errors` clause: there every possible error
must be handled or proven impossible. A `fail NAME` statement is held to
the same rule: it needs `errors NAME` on the enclosing function (at the
top level it is never admitted). A handler for an error the call cannot
produce is accepted and never runs.

== WHY
"Known failure -> compile-time rejection; unknown failure -> declared
runtime error completion; never an undeclared one"
(EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md). An error is a completion of the call, not
a value: there is no Result to unwrap and nothing that could drop a
failure silently. A function's `errors` clause is part of its contract,
like its parameter and result types, so every caller sees what can go
wrong and decides where each failure is answered.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR, at the call, naming every error of the call
that is left (one diagnostic per call):

    this call may produce the declared error "Negative", which is neither
    handled here nor admitted by the enclosing function's own "errors"
    declaration

A standard-library call names itself (`this call of "std::list::at" may
produce ...`; proof.list-bounds lists the forms the index proof
recognizes). A `fail` that nothing admits is `"fail Bad" is not admitted
here: the enclosing function does not declare "errors Bad"`.

@example reject
error Negative

fn check(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn use(n: int) -> int:
    check(n)

use(3)
@caption Neither handled nor admitted
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 9:5
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Decide where the failure belongs. Handle it where a sensible outcome
exists (a fallback value, a `return`, or a `fail` of another error);
admit it in the function's `errors` clause when the caller should decide;
or restate the code so that the compiler proves the error impossible (a
guard before the call, or a recognized pattern: proof.list-bounds).

@example accept
error Negative

fn check(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn or_zero(n: int) -> int:
    check(n):
        on Negative:
            0

fn plus_one(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    check(n) + 1

[or_zero(-3), plus_one(3)]
@caption Handled in or_zero, admitted by plus_one
@value [0, 4]
@end

@topic rule.known-error
title: A call that always fails is rejected, even with a handler
summary: A call the compiler proves can never complete normally under the facts at that call is KNOWN-ERROR; a handler does not make it legal.
index: rules
alias: a handler does not make a statically known failure legal; call that always fails
keywords: known error, always fails, handler, literal argument, statically decided, negative test
related: concept.known-errors concept.negative-tests concept.static-compile proof.completions rule.unhandled-errors stdlib.std::list::get error.known-error
docs: STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md

== THE RULE
For every call of an exactly known function, the compiler computes from
the facts it has about the arguments at that call (literal values, ranges
established by guards, declared types) which declared errors the call can
produce and whether it can complete normally at all. A call that cannot
complete normally -- it always produces a declared error -- is rejected,
whether or not it is handled. Only a call that may succeed is legal; its
possible errors then follow rule.unhandled-errors.

`[1, 2].at(5)` is such a call, and so is `check(-3)` for a `check` that
fails on every negative argument. The same call inside a function, with
the argument a parameter, is an ordinary call that may fail: the analysis
of a function does not take its callers' arguments into account.

== WHY
"A handler is not a way to encode an `if` for a statically known
condition" (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md). When the compiler knows that a
call fails, the failure is a defect it can report now; writing the
outcome directly says the same thing without a call that cannot succeed.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR, at the call:

    this call can never complete normally under the facts proven for its
    arguments here -- it always produces the declared error(s) Negative;
    a handler does not make a statically known failure legal

@example reject
error Negative

fn check(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

check(-3):
    on Negative:
        0
@caption A call that always fails, handled
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 8:1
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Write the outcome the failure would have produced (here, `0`). When the
failing input is data -- a fallback path, a test of the failure -- pass it
through a function parameter: the call inside the function may fail, and its
handler is ordinary (concept.negative-tests). For a List read with a
fallback, std::list::get(xs, i, fallback) is the handled read written once.
To test that a program is rejected at compile time, use `static compile:`
(concept.static-compile).

@example accept
error Negative

fn check(n: int) -> int errors Negative:
    if n < 0:
        fail Negative
    n

fn checked_or_zero(n: int) -> int:
    check(n):
        on Negative:
            0

[checked_or_zero(-3), checked_or_zero(4)]
@caption The failing input passed through a parameter
@value [0, 4]
@end

@topic rule.method-requires-import
title: Method syntax applies only functions visible by name
summary: receiver.f(args) is the call f(receiver, args) and needs a function f visible at the call; nothing is looked up through the receiver's type, so s.concat("x") needs import std::str.
index: rules
alias: method call needs import; method not found on str; why does s.concat need import std::str
keywords: method, import, visible, receiver, concat, str, list, namespace, MISSING-IMPORT
related: concept.method-sugar concept.imports rule.method-resolution rule.imports-not-transitive principle.explicit-dependencies stdlib.module.std::str stdlib.std::str::concat error.missing-import
docs: METHOD-SUGAR.md IMPORTS.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md

== THE RULE
`receiver.f(a, b)` is another spelling of the ordinary call
`f(receiver, a, b)`, allowed exactly when a function named `f` is visible
at the call:

  * a function or binding of the file, defined above the call, or a
    parameter or local of an enclosing scope;
  * a root native (`argv`, `hash`, `mod`, ...), visible everywhere;
  * a member `f` of a namespace the file imports directly (`import std::str`
    makes `std::str::concat` a candidate for `.concat(...)`).

Nothing is looked up through the receiver's type: a `str` value does not
"have" the functions of `str`. Without `import std::str`, `s.concat("xy")`
finds no `concat`, and neither does `std::str::concat(s, "xy")`. An import
injects no unqualified name: after `import std::str`, `concat(s, "xy")` is
still an unbound name; `s.concat("xy")` and `std::str::concat(s, "xy")` are
the two spellings.

== WHY
Method syntax is parasitic on ordinary function calls (METHOD-SUGAR.md):
no method declaration, no method table, no search by receiver type. It
asks "is there a function f visible here, and would f(x, ...) be valid?".
Visibility comes from the file's own header, the one place a file
declares what it depends on (IMPORTS.md), so the functions a file can
reach -- with either spelling -- are those its imports name.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-IMPORT, at the method name, when an unimported
namespace has a function the call would apply:

    namespace "std::str" is not imported; add `import std::str` to use
    `std::str::concat` (the method-style call ".concat(...)" applies only a
    function visible by that name)

The qualified spelling reports SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT. When no
known namespace has such a function, the call is reported as the
projection of a field (NOT-A-STRUCT or UNKNOWN-FIELD). (Before
DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md, section 1, a failed method call was typed `any`
and surfaced as a TYPE error far from the call.)

@example reject
s = "ab"
s.concat("xy")
@caption No import of str
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-IMPORT
@at 2:3
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Import the namespace whose function you mean.

@example accept
import std::str

s = "ab"
s.concat("xy")
@caption import std::str makes std::str::concat a candidate
@value "abxy"
@end

@topic rule.method-resolution
title: Method calls: exactly one visible candidate must fit
summary: When several visible functions share a method name, each is tried as the ordinary call; exactly one must be valid, and there is no precedence between them.
index: rules
alias: method candidates; which function does x.f() call; method overload
keywords: method, candidate, ambiguous, no applicable, field, callable field, precedence, overload, nomethod
related: concept.method-sugar concept.nomethod rule.method-requires-import rule.typed-parameters error.ambiguous-method-call error.no-applicable-method error.nomethod-call
docs: METHOD-SUGAR.md WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md

== THE RULE
The candidates of `x.f(args)` are the distinct functions named `f` visible
at the call: the lexical one, if any, and the member `f` of each directly
imported namespace. Each is tried as the ordinary call `f(x, args)`:

  * exactly one is valid: the call is that function's call;
  * several are valid: the call is ambiguous -- there is no precedence by
    import order, by "local first" or by "standard library first";
  * none is valid: no candidate applies, and the diagnostic lists why each
    fails (with a single candidate, it is that call's own diagnostic).

A candidate is valid when its call has no diagnostic of its own (a
declared parameter type or an inferred contract the receiver cannot
prove, an invalid flag); a declared error nobody handles is not evidence
against it. A function of another arity, and a `nomethod fn`, are no
candidates at all.

A struct field interferes only when it may hold a function: if the
receiver's struct has a field `f` whose value may be callable and a
function `f` is visible, `x.f()` is ambiguous; `(x.f)()` calls the field
and `f(x)` the function. A field of a non-callable type (an `int` field
named like a function) is no candidate.

== WHY
Botlish has no overloading: a qualified spelling is one exact function and
a name denotes one binding. A method name is looked up in several places
at once, so the sugar applies its one rule to each candidate and refuses
to guess. Because nothing is preferred, adding an import can make a call
ambiguous, but it can never silently change which function a valid call
runs.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL, at the method name:

    method-style call "length" is ambiguous: more than one visible
    function accepts the receiver (length, std::list::length); there is no
    precedence between them: call the one you mean explicitly, e.g.
    length(receiver, ...)

and CORE SEMANTIC NO-APPLICABLE-METHOD, `method-style call "length": none
of the visible functions of that name accepts the receiver (...)`.

@example reject
import std::list

fn length(xs):
    0

ys = [1, 2, 3]
ys.length()
@caption Two candidates accept a List
@error CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-METHOD-CALL
@at 7:4
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Call the function you mean in its ordinary spelling (`std::list::length(ys)`
or `length(ys)`), rename the local function, or declare its parameter type
so that only one candidate accepts the receiver.

@example accept
import std::list

fn length(s: str) -> int:
    0

xs = [1, 2, 3]
xs.length()
@caption Only std::list::length accepts a List
@value 3
@end

@topic rule.immutable-bindings
title: A name is bound once per scope; there is no assignment
summary: "x = e" binds a new immutable name, at most once per scope; a nested scope may shadow it, and there is no assignment, "+=", field or element assignment.
index: rules errors
alias: rebind a variable; reassign a name; duplicate binding; field assignment; only a name can be bound
keywords: binding, immutable, assignment, shadowing, duplicate, scope, accumulate, +=
related: concept.bindings concept.values rule.forward-references capability.assignment principle.immutability concept.mutable-vector error.duplicate
docs: README.md STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | only a name can be bound with "="*

== THE RULE
`name = expression` creates a new binding whose value never changes. It
is not assignment:

  * a name is bound at most once in a scope; a function's parameters and
    its body are one scope, so a body cannot rebind a parameter;
  * a nested scope (an `if` branch, a loop body, a handler, a nested
    function) may bind the same name again; that binding shadows the
    outer one until the nested scope ends, and the outer one is unchanged;
  * only a name can be bound: there is no field assignment (`p.x = 2`), no
    element assignment (`xs[0] = 1`), no `+=` and no `++`.

Struct, List and String values are immutable too: a "changed" value is a
new value. MutableVector and MutableArray change through their own
operations on a place, never through `=`.

A common surprise: `total = total + i` in a loop body binds a new `total`
in the body on every iteration. The outer `total` keeps its value.

== WHY
A binding's value is fixed where it is established, so a fact the
compiler proves about it -- its type, its range -- stays true wherever the
binding is visible from then on, and a reader never has to trace later
writes to know what a name means. State that changes is a separate,
explicit kind of value (MutableVector, MutableArray) whose changes are
calls.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE, at the second binding:

    duplicate binding "x" in the same lexical scope

(for parameters, `... (block parameters)`), and SURFACE SYNTAX for a
target that is not a name:

    only a name can be bound with "="

`x += 1` is `expected an expression, found "="`.

@example reject
price = 100
price = price - 15
price
@caption A second binding in one scope
@error CORE SEMANTIC DUPLICATE
@at 2:1
@end

@example reject
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

p = Point {x: 1, y: 2}
p.x = 5
p
@caption Field assignment
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 6:5
@message only a name can be bound with "="
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Give each new value its own name (`discounted = price - 15`). Build a
"changed" struct as a new construction. Accumulate with a collecting loop
or with recursion that passes the running value as a parameter. Use
MutableVector when a growing sequence really is the model
(concept.mutable-vector).

@example accept
fn sum_to(n: int, acc: int) -> int:
    if n == 0:
        return acc
    sum_to(n - 1, acc + n)

sum_to(4, 0)
@caption An accumulator passed as a parameter
@value 10
@end

@example accept
total = 1
loop i from 0 to 3:
    total = total + i
total
@caption Shadowing leaves the outer total unchanged
@value 1
@end

@topic rule.no-callable-types
title: No type is callable
summary: A type declaration declares a type and nothing callable: T(x), T?(x), x.T?() and ns::T? are unknown names unless the program declares a function of that name.
index: rules
alias: type constructor call; type predicate; call a type as a function; convert to a domain type
keywords: type, constructor, predicate, converter, domain, refinement, Small?, unbound
related: concept.integer-domains concept.refinements concept.structs rule.struct-construction rule.typed-parameters proof.ranges proof.refinements error.unbound
docs: ELIMINATE-AUTOMATIC-TYPE-PREDICATES.md EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md REFINEMENT-VALUES.md

== THE RULE
No type declaration -- `type`, `refined type`, `struct`, `enum`,
`layout`, `context struct` -- creates anything callable: no constructor
function and no predicate. `Small(3)`, `Small?(3)`, `x.Small?()` and
`geo::Small?(x)` name functions that do not exist unless the program
declares them. Built-in type names are not functions either (`int(3)` is
an unbound name). A struct value is made by its construction syntax,
`Point {x: 1, y: 2}` (rule.struct-construction).

A value enters an integer domain in one of two ordinary ways:

  * a converter: a function whose declared result is the domain, checked
    like any result (`fn to_small(n: int) -> Small errors OutOfRange:`);
  * comparisons: on the edge where `0 <= n and n <= 10` holds, `n` is
    proven to be a `Small` (proof.ranges).

A refinement is entered through its owner's `proves` predicates and
validators (concept.refinements).

== WHY
Automatic type predicates (`Small?`) and constructor calls (`Byte(x)`) once
existed and were removed (ELIMINATE-AUTOMATIC-TYPE-PREDICATES.md): a
constructor raised an undeclared run-time error, and a predicate was
lowered differently by every backend and could not be compiled natively
in exactly the case where a run-time test is worth anything. A converter
is an ordinary function whose failure is a declared error in its `errors`
clause, and a comparison is an ordinary condition: both are checked like
any other code.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND, at the name: `unbound name "Small"` (or
`"Small?"`); with method syntax, NOT-A-STRUCT, because `x.Small?()` is
then read as the projection of a field.

@example reject
type Small = Int in 0..10

Small(3)
@caption A type is not a function
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 3:1
@message unbound name "Small"
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Write a converter with a declared error, or establish the range with
comparisons before the use.

@example accept
type Small = Int in 0..10
error OutOfRange

fn to_small(n: int) -> Small errors OutOfRange:
    if n < 0 or n > 10:
        fail OutOfRange
    n

fn twice(x: Small) -> int:
    x * 2

twice(to_small(4))
@caption A converter function
@value 8
@end

@topic rule.struct-construction
title: A struct construction gives every field exactly once
summary: Name {field: value, ...} names every declared field exactly once, in any order, with no defaults and no unknown fields, each value proven to satisfy its field's type.
index: rules
alias: missing field in construction; struct default values; construct a struct; positional struct construction
keywords: struct, construction, field, default, missing field, unknown field, duplicate field, shorthand
related: concept.structs concept.opaque-structs concept.destructuring rule.no-callable-types rule.opaque-authority rule.immutable-bindings error.missing-field error.unknown-field error.duplicate-field
docs: STRUCTS.md OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md

== THE RULE
`Point {x: 1, y: 2}` constructs a value of the declared struct `Point`:

  * every declared field is given exactly once; the order is free;
  * there are no defaults, no optional fields and no unknown fields;
  * each value must be proven to satisfy its field's declared type, with
    the proof a call argument gets for a declared parameter
    (rule.typed-parameters); no run-time check is inserted;
  * fields are always written `name: value`: there is no shorthand
    `Point {x, y}` (that form exists only in destructuring) and no
    positional form `Point(1, 2)` (rule.no-callable-types).

A module's struct is constructed as `geo::Point {...}` after `import geo`;
an opaque struct only inside its own module (rule.opaque-authority). An
anonymous struct `{x: 1, y: 2}` has exactly the fields written. Struct
values are immutable: a value with one field changed is a new
construction that names every field.

== WHY
A struct is a fixed set of named fields whose shape the compiler knows, so
a construction is complete, exact and statically checked (STRUCTS.md): one
construction determines every field, and a projection can never fail.
With no defaults, adding a field to a declaration turns every construction
that lacks it into an error instead of silently filling it in.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-FIELD, at the construction:

    the Point construction is missing field "y" (every declared field must
    be given exactly once; there are no defaults)

and UNKNOWN-FIELD (`struct Point has no field "z" (declared fields: x,
y)`), DUPLICATE-FIELD, or TYPE for a value that is not proven to satisfy
its field's type.

@example reject
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

Point {x: 1}
@caption A missing field
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-FIELD
@at 5:1
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Give every field; to derive a value from another, construct a new one and
copy the fields that stay.

@example accept
struct Point:
    x: int
    y: int

fn moved_right(p: Point, by: int) -> Point:
    Point {y: p.y, x: p.x + by}

Point {x: 1, y: 2}.moved_right(3)
@caption Every field once, in any order
@value Point {x: 4, y: 2}
@end

@topic rule.import-type
title: import type imports type declarations and enums, not structs
summary: import type NS::T binds the short name T in type positions for NS's type declarations, refinements, traits and enums; a struct is spelled NS::Struct after import NS.
index: rules
alias: import type; import type of a struct; short name for a module's type; import type NS::T
keywords: import type, short name, struct, enum, type declaration, refinement, trait, NOT-A-TYPE
related: concept.imports concept.enums concept.structs concept.integer-domains rule.imports-not-transitive capability.struct-type-import error.not-a-type error.type-import-collision
docs: IMPORTS.md ENUMS.md STRUCTS.md

== THE RULE
`import type NS::T` binds the short name `T`, in this file's type
positions, for one of these declarations of namespace NS:

  * a `type` declaration (an integer domain, `type Byte = Int in 0..255`)
    or a `refined type`;
  * a trait, including a context trait (`import type std::io::IO`);
  * an enum, whose cases are then written `T::Case`.

It does not import a struct, a layout, an error, a function or an intrinsic
type of the namespace (the `std::ffi::c` scalars), and it creates no value
binding. It authorizes nothing else: `NS::...` references, short or not,
still need `import NS` (even `NS::T` itself). A struct keeps its qualified
spelling, `NS::Struct`, in annotations and constructions alike, after
`import NS`. The short name may not collide with a type the file declares, a
built-in type or another imported short name (TYPE-IMPORT-COLLISION).

== WHY
The type import is deliberately narrow (IMPORTS.md): it names one type's
last component for type positions only, it is not an alias (`T` and
`NS::T` are one canonical type), and it authorizes nothing else of NS.
Structs keep their qualified spelling after `import NS` (IMPORTS.md), and
so do layouts; capability.struct-type-import has the alternatives.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-TYPE, at the imported name:

    "std::abi::U8" is a struct, not a source-defined `type`: `import type`
    imports `type` declarations only; spell the struct std::abi::U8 after
    `import std::abi`

(with its own wording for a layout, an error, a function and an
intrinsic type).

@example reject
import type std::abi::U8

std::abi::U8 {value: 3}
@caption std::abi::U8 is a struct
@error SURFACE MODULE NOT-A-TYPE
@at 1:23
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Import the namespace and spell the struct qualified; use `import type` for
domains, refinements, traits and enums.

@example accept
import std::abi

v = std::abi::U8 {value: 3}
v.value
@caption A struct spelled qualified after import std::abi
@value 3
@end

@example accept
import type std::byte::Byte
import std::compiler
import type std::compiler::DiagnosticCode

fn twice(b: Byte) -> int:
    b * 2

c = DiagnosticCode::Type
[twice(3), c == std::compiler::DiagnosticCode::Type]
@caption Short names: a domain and an enum
@value [6, true]
@end

@topic rule.no-list-destructuring
title: Lists are not destructured positionally
summary: [a, b] = value is LIST-DESTRUCTURING: a List is a sequence; several results of different meaning are a struct, destructured by field name.
index: rules
alias: unpack a list; tuple unpacking; positional destructuring
keywords: destructuring, list, tuple, pair, struct, named fields, positional
related: concept.destructuring concept.lists concept.structs principle.name-the-parts warning.fixed-arity-list-return error.list-destructuring
docs: STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md MULTI-VALUE-RESULTS.md

== THE RULE
`[a, b] = value`, and a List pattern nested in a struct pattern (`{pair: [a,
b]} = v`), are not binding forms. A List is the type of a sequence of
values; a function with several results of different meaning returns a
struct, and the caller binds its fields by name with struct destructuring,
`{first, second} = result`. There is no tuple or pair type. Elements of a
List are read with `std::list::at` (with its proof obligations,
proof.list-bounds) or by iterating it.

== WHY
"Destructuring is not pattern matching. It is one struct value, selected
fields read by name" (STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md). Positions carry no meaning
a reader can check, while field names do; a struct's fields are known
statically, so struct destructuring cannot fail, while a positional
binding would depend on the List's length. This is a language policy,
not a missing feature.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX, with the parser code LIST-DESTRUCTURING:

    LIST-DESTRUCTURING: positional List destructuring is not supported;
    use a struct value with named fields instead (...)

@example reject
pair = [1, 2]
[a, b] = pair
a + b
@caption Positional destructuring of a List
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:1
@message LIST-DESTRUCTURING: *
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Return a struct and destructure it by name.

@example accept
struct Split:
    first: int
    second: int

fn split(n: int) -> Split:
    Split {first: n, second: n * 2}

{first, second} = split(3)
first + second
@caption A struct result, destructured by field name
@value 9
@end

@topic rule.equality
title: What == compares, and what has no equality
summary: == and != compare values structurally (Lists element-wise, structs by shape and fields, enums by enum and case); functions and mutable collections have no equality; affine values are rejected.
index: rules
alias: compare two values; structural equality; compare functions; compare structs
keywords: ==, !=, equality, compare, struct, list, enum, function, MutableVector, MutableArray, hash, ordering
related: concept.values concept.structs concept.enums concept.affine concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array rule.comparison-chains error.equality error.affine-equality-unsupported
docs: STRUCTS.md ENUMS.md OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md MUTABLE-VECTOR.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md AFFINE-VALUES.md

== THE RULE
`a == b` and `a != b` compare values, never identities:

  * Int, str, bool, unit and UnicodeChar compare by value; values of
    different kinds are unequal, never converted (`1 == "one"` and
    `'a' == "a"` are false, not errors);
  * a List equals a List of the same length with equal elements;
  * a struct equals a struct of the same shape -- the same named
    declaration, or the same anonymous field set -- whose fields are all
    equal; a named struct never equals an anonymous one or another
    declaration's value, whatever their fields;
  * an enum case equals the same case of the same enum; same-named cases
    of two enums are unequal;
  * an ImmutableSet equals a set with the same members, in any order;
  * an opaque struct is compared whole, outside its module too; Bytes and
    MutableBytes by their bytes;
  * a refined value compares as its carrier.

Function values (and natives), MutableVector and MutableArray, and any
value containing one, have no equality: comparing them fails at run time
with EQUALITY, on every backend. Affine values (a coroutine handle, an
aggregate that owns one, an `std::ffi::c` pointer) are rejected at compile
time. `hash` follows the same rules. The ordering comparisons `<`, `<=`,
`>` and `>=` are defined for Int (and integer domains) only; another
operand is a TYPE error, which today is raised when the comparison runs
(`"ab" < "cd"` compiles).

== WHY
Equality of functions would require identity semantics, which are out of
scope (core/value.tcl). A MutableVector's or MutableArray's header
is the identity of one changing value, and "a header's identity is never a
value's", so none was defined for them. An affine value owns something
(a suspended coroutine); comparing or hashing it would observe the
identity of what it owns (AFFINE-VALUES.md).

== WHAT IS REPORTED
For an affine operand, CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED at the
operand. For a function or a mutable collection, CORE SEMANTIC EQUALITY at
run time (the program compiles):

    == is not defined for callables: <block (x)> == <block (x)>

(the message says "callables" for a MutableVector or MutableArray too).

@example reject
fn counter():
    yield 1
    2

coroutine {step} = counter()
step == step
@caption A coroutine handle
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED
@at 6:1
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Compare the data you mean: an id or enum field instead of a function, the
elements of a mutable collection, the unrestricted fields of a struct
that owns an affine value.

@example accept
struct P:
    x: int

enum Color:
    Red, Green

[
    P {x: 1} == P {x: 1},
    [1, [2]] == [1, [2]],
    1 == "one",
    P {x: 1} == {x: 1},
    Color::Red != Color::Green,
]
@caption Structural equality
@value [true, true, false, false, true]
@end

@topic rule.comparison-chains
title: Comparisons do not chain
summary: A comparison operator takes two operands; a < b < c and a == b == c are syntax errors; write a < b and b < c.
index: rules errors
alias: chained comparison; a < b < c; range check between two bounds
keywords: comparison, chain, <, <=, ==, between, and, precedence
related: concept.booleans concept.integers rule.equality concept.syntax error.surface-syntax
docs: README.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | comparison chaining is not supported*

== THE RULE
`==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` compare two operands and do not
associate: a comparison cannot be an operand of another comparison without
parentheses. `lo <= x <= hi` is a syntax error; write `lo <= x and
x <= hi`. Comparisons bind more loosely than `+`, `-` and `*` and more
tightly than `not`, `and` and `or`, so `a + 1 < b * 2 and b < c` needs no
parentheses. A parenthesized comparison is an ordinary Bool operand:
`(a < b) == ok` is legal.

== WHY
The chained form has two conventional meanings: in mathematics `a < b < c`
is `a < b and b < c`, in C-like languages it is `(a < b) < c`, which
compares a Bool with c. Botlish accepts neither silently; the program says
which one it means.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX, at the second comparison operator:

    comparison chaining is not supported

@example reject
fn between?(x: int, lo: int, hi: int) -> bool:
    lo <= x <= hi

0
@caption A chained comparison
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:13
@message comparison chaining is not supported
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Join the two comparisons with `and`.

@example accept
fn between?(x: int, lo: int, hi: int) -> bool:
    lo <= x and x <= hi

[5.between?(1, 10), 0.between?(1, 10)]
@caption Two comparisons joined with and
@value [true, false]
@end

@topic rule.indentation
title: Blocks are indented with spaces
summary: A block follows a line ending in ":" on the next lines, indented deeper with spaces; tabs in indentation, inconsistent dedents and stray indentation are syntax errors.
index: rules errors
alias: tab used for indentation; indentation error; inconsistent dedent; indented block; tabs vs spaces
keywords: indentation, indent, dedent, tab, spaces, block, colon, layout, brackets
related: concept.syntax rule.if-placement rule.loop-placement rule.names error.surface-syntax
docs: README.md DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | tab used for indentation*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | inconsistent dedent*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | unexpected indentation*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected an indented block*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected a new line and an indented block after ":"*

== THE RULE
Block structure is layout:

  * a block -- a function body, an `if`/`elif`/`else` branch, a loop body,
    a handler, the fields of a struct, error or enum declaration, a
    `static compile:` body -- follows a line ending in `:` and starts on
    the next line, indented deeper than that line; there are no one-line
    blocks (`if c: x` on one line is an error);
  * indentation is counted in spaces; any width works (the standard
    library uses 4), and every line of a block has the same indentation;
  * a line indented less than the one before must return exactly to an
    enclosing level;
  * a tab character in indentation is an error (a tab between tokens is
    ordinary whitespace);
  * blank lines and comment-only lines do not count;
  * inside `( )`, `[ ]` and `{ }` newlines and indentation are ignored, so
    a long call, list or struct literal may span lines freely.

== WHY
The indentation is the structure the parser sees, so it must be the
structure a reader sees. A tab's width depends on the editor: a file that
mixes tabs and spaces can look like one structure and parse as another, so
indentation is spaces only.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX, at the offending line:

    tab used for indentation
    inconsistent dedent: indentation does not match any enclosing level
    unexpected indentation
    expected an indented block, found ...
    expected a new line and an indented block after ":", found ...

@example reject
fn f(x):
	x

f(1)
@caption A tab in indentation
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:1
@message tab used for indentation
@end

@example reject
fn f(x):
    if x:
        1
      else:
        2

f(true)
@caption A dedent to no enclosing level
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:7
@message inconsistent dedent*
@end

@example reject
x = 1
    y = 2
y
@caption Indentation without a block to open
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:5
@message unexpected indentation
@end

@example reject
fn f(x):
x

f(1)
@caption A body that is not indented
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:1
@message expected an indented block*
@end

@example reject
fn f(n: int) -> int:
    if n > 0: return 1
    0

f(1)
@caption A block on its colon's line
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:15
@message expected a new line and an indented block after ":"*
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Set the editor to insert spaces, re-indent the block consistently, and
give every block its own lines.

@example accept
fn f(n: int) -> int:
  if n > 0:
      return 1
  0

xs = [
1,
        2]

[f(2), f(-2), xs]
@caption Any consistent width; free layout in brackets
@value [1, 0, [1, 2]]
@end

@topic rule.names
title: How names are spelled
summary: A name is letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit, optionally ending in one "?"; "-" is the minus operator and a "?" anywhere else is a syntax error.
index: rules errors
alias: name spelling; identifier syntax; question mark in a name; hyphen in a name; dash in a function name
keywords: name, identifier, ?, predicate, hyphen, dash, underscore, keyword, snake_case, unicode
related: concept.syntax principle.predicate-naming warning.proves-naming rule.if-placement rule.indentation error.surface-syntax error.unbound
docs: README.md R2A2-TRAILING-QUESTION-IDENTIFIERS.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | *already ends in "?"*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | unexpected character "?"*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected "(" after the function name, found "-"*

== THE RULE
A name is `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`, optionally followed by one `?`:

  * `?` may only end a name, once: `empty?` and `ready?` are names,
    `ok??` and `is?ok` are not. By convention a name ending in `?` is a
    predicate returning `bool` (principle.predicate-naming);
  * `-` is the minus operator: `my-value` is `my - value`, so names use
    underscores (`item_count`);
  * letters are ASCII only (`café` is not a name);
  * by convention functions and values are snake_case
    (`encode_utf8`), declared types PascalCase (`MutableVector`);
  * these words are reserved: fn if elif else loop return break continue
    true false unit and or not type struct error errors fail on yield.
    Words such as `import`, `with`, `context`, `flags`, `opaque`,
    `nomethod`, `static`, `coroutine` and `enum` are contextual: ordinary
    names wherever their construct cannot start.

== WHY
`?` at the end of a name is the predicate idiom (`emailish?`), kept
unambiguous by allowing it nowhere else
(R2A2-TRAILING-QUESTION-IDENTIFIERS.md); there is no `?:` operator for it
to be confused with. `-` stays an operator, so `a-b` means subtraction
whatever the spacing; one consequence is that the few internal natives
with `-` in their names cannot be named from source at all.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE SYNTAX:

    "ok?" already ends in "?": an identifier can end in at most one "?",
    with nothing directly after it
    unexpected character "?"                     (a "?" not ending a name)
    expected "(" after the function name, found "-"   (fn my-name(...))

A hyphenated name elsewhere is read as a subtraction: in a binding it is
`only a name can be bound with "="`, in an expression an `unbound name`
for one of its halves.

@example reject
fn ok??(x):
    x

ok??(1)
@caption Two question marks
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:7
@message *already ends in "?"*
@end

@example reject
n = 3
big = n > 2 ? 1 : 0
big
@caption A ? that does not end a name
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:13
@message unexpected character "?"
@end

@example reject
fn item-count(xs):
    0

0
@caption A hyphen in a function name
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:8
@message expected "(" after the function name, found "-"
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Use underscores between words and at most one `?`, at the end. For a
conditional value, use an `if` (rule.if-placement).

@example accept
fn ready?(n: int) -> bool:
    n > 2

item_count_2 = 3
_hidden = item_count_2.ready?()
_hidden
@caption Underscores, digits and one trailing ?
@value true
@end

@topic rule.opaque-authority
title: Only the declaring module builds or inspects an opaque struct
summary: An opaque struct's representation belongs to the module that declares it: only that module may construct it or read and destructure its fields; everyone else uses its operations.
index: rules
alias: opaque struct field access; construct an opaque struct; representation ownership
keywords: opaque, struct, module, construction, projection, destructuring, authority, Bytes
related: concept.opaque-structs concept.modules rule.struct-construction concept.private stdlib.std::abi::bytes::from_list error.opaque-construction error.opaque-representation
docs: OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md

== THE RULE
`opaque struct Token:` declares an ordinary struct whose representation
belongs to the module that declares it (an entry program owns its own):

  * only the owner may construct it (`token::Token {...}`), project its
    fields (`t.value`) or destructure it (`{value} = t`);
  * everyone may name the type, pass, store and return its values,
    compare and hash them whole, and call the owner's functions on them,
    with method syntax too;
  * authority is the declaring module exactly: no import, parent or child
    module, and no struct that holds the value grants it.

== WHY
An opaque struct is representation ownership, not object-oriented privacy
(OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md): the module that owns `std::abi::bytes::Bytes` keeps the
invariants of its storage, because nothing else can manufacture or take
apart a value. The check is static authority, never a run-time test, and
the compiler still sees the fields when it optimizes.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION for a field read or a destructuring
outside the owner:

    field access on std::abi::bytes::Bytes is not available outside module
    "std::abi::bytes"; std::abi::bytes::Bytes has an opaque representation, so its
    fields can be neither projected nor destructured here (use the public
    operations provided by that module)

and CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-CONSTRUCTION for a construction.

@example reject
import std::abi::bytes
import std::str

data = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("hi"))
data.storage
@caption A field read outside the owner
@error CORE SEMANTIC OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION
@at 5:6
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Use the operations the owning module provides; in your own module, write
the functions other code needs.

@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::str

data = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("hi"))
n = std::abi::bytes::length(data)
n.value
@caption The owner's operations, and whole-value use
@value 2
@end

@topic rule.context-installation
title: Installing a context: at the entry top level, once, before use
summary: with context EXPR installs a context only at the entry program's top level, at most once per context type, and every top-level call must come after the installations it needs.
index: rules
alias: where to write with context; install a context; context installed too late
keywords: context, with context, install, entry program, top level, requirement, order, MISSING-CONTEXT
related: concept.contexts concept.context-traits proof.contexts rule.module-bindings error.missing-context error.duplicate-context error.context-installation-unsupported
docs: CONTEXTS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md

== THE RULE
`with context EXPR` installs the value of EXPR, whose type is exactly one
context struct, as that type's context from that statement on:

  * only in the entry program's top-level scope, as a statement: not in a
    function, a branch, a loop, a handler or a module;
  * at most once per context type: no second installation, no nested or
    overriding one;
  * every top-level call must come after the installation of every context
    its callee requires -- the contexts its own context parameters name
    and those of everything it calls -- in source order.

A function names a context only where it uses one (`fn stamp(x, context
clock: Clock)`); its callers need nothing in their signatures, and the
compiler derives each requirement from the call graph. A function that
requires a context cannot become a value, an alias aside
(CONTEXT-FUNCTION-VALUE).

== WHY
A context is a static dependency of the execution environment
(CONTEXTS.md): the compiler proves, statement by statement, that each call
runs after its contexts exist, and names the call chain that needs one
when it does not. With one installation per type at the top level, each
context has one statically known value, which native code reads from one
fixed slot: no hidden argument, no lookup.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT, at the call, with the chain that needs the
context:

    Clock is required by this call but is not installed here (install it
    with "with context EXPR" before the call):
        twice
         -> stamp
            directly requires Clock

DUPLICATE-CONTEXT for a second installation, and
CONTEXT-INSTALLATION-UNSUPPORTED for one anywhere else.

@example reject
context struct Clock:
    now: int

fn stamp(x: int, context clock: Clock) -> int:
    x + clock.now

fn twice(x: int) -> int:
    stamp(x) * 2

r = twice(5)
with context Clock {now: 100}
r
@caption A call before its installation
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT
@at 10:5
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Install contexts near the top of the entry program, before the first call
that needs them; move code that runs before the installation into
functions called after it.

@example accept
context struct Clock:
    now: int

fn stamp(x: int, context clock: Clock) -> int:
    x + clock.now

fn twice(x: int) -> int:
    stamp(x) * 2

with context Clock {now: 100}
twice(5)
@caption Installed once, before the calls
@value 210
@end

@topic rule.affine-use
title: An affine value has one owner: no use after a move
summary: Binding, passing, returning or storing an affine value (a coroutine handle, or a struct or List owning one) moves it; the old name may not be used again.
index: rules
alias: moved coroutine handle; copy a coroutine handle; affine ownership rule
keywords: affine, move, owner, coroutine, handle, resume, release, USE-AFTER-MOVE
related: concept.affine concept.coroutines proof.ownership rule.equality error.use-after-move error.affine-capture-unsupported
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md

== THE RULE
An affine value -- a coroutine handle, an `std::ffi::c` pointer, and every
struct, anonymous struct or List that owns one -- has one usable owner:

  * binding it to another name, passing it as an argument, returning it,
    or storing it in a struct field or List element moves it; the old
    owner is dead from then on, and any later use of it is an error;
  * resuming a coroutine (`step()`, `step(message)`) uses the handle
    without moving it;
  * there is no copy or clone, and a nested function cannot capture an
    affine value;
  * an affine value may be dropped unused; it is released where its owner
    dies (after its last use).

== WHY
An affine value owns something that must be released exactly once -- a
suspended coroutine's stack and frames. A second owner could resume or
release the same execution, so ownership is decided statically: there is
no reference count, moved flag or ownership object at run time
(AFFINE-VALUES.md).

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC USE-AFTER-MOVE, at the use, with where it moved:

    `step` was used after its coroutine value was moved

    moved here:
        other = step  (program.bot:6:1)

AFFINE-CAPTURE-UNSUPPORTED for a capture by a nested function, and
AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED for a comparison (rule.equality).

@example reject
fn counter():
    yield 1
    yield 2
    3

coroutine {step, first} = counter()
other = step
step()
@caption The old name used after the move
@error CORE SEMANTIC USE-AFTER-MOVE
@at 8:1
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Use the new owner. A function that needs the value only for a while can
take it as a parameter and return it with its result; a struct that owns
it moves as a whole, and destructuring moves its fields out.

@example accept
fn counter():
    yield 1
    yield 2
    3

coroutine {step, first} = counter()
other = step
a = other()
b = other()
[first, a, b]
@caption The new owner resumes the coroutine
@value [1, 2, 3]
@end

@example accept
fn counter():
    yield 1
    yield 2
    3

fn advance(h: Coroutine{args: [], return: int, errors: []}):
    v = h()
    {value: v, step: h}

coroutine {step, first} = counter()
{value, step: again} = advance(step)
[first, value, again()]
@caption Lent to a function that returns it with its result
@value [1, 2, 3]
@end

@topic rule.control-placement
title: Where break, continue and return may appear
summary: break and continue belong in a loop body of the same function, return in a function body; none crosses a function boundary, and break takes no value.
index: rules errors
alias: break inside a nested function; break with a value; return a value from a loop
keywords: break, continue, return, loop, function, top level, nested function, value
related: concept.loops concept.functions rule.if-placement rule.loop-placement error.break-outside-loop error.continue-outside-loop error.return-outside-callable
docs: README.md PAYLOAD-FREE-BREAK.md COLLECTING-LOOPS.md
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | "break" does not take a value*

== THE RULE
  * `break` and `continue` must be inside a loop body of the same
    function. `if` branches and handlers inside the loop count as the
    loop's body; a nested function's body does not, even when it is
    defined inside the loop.
  * `return` must be inside a function body: not at the program's top
    level and not in a module's top level.
  * `break` takes no value. In a collecting loop it ends the loop with the
    List collected so far; a bare `loop:` evaluates to `unit`. `continue`
    in a collecting loop contributes nothing to the List.

== WHY
A nested function is a value that may run later, after the loop around
its definition has finished, so a `break` inside it would have no loop to
leave (README section 5, "Control placement"). `break` answers one
question, whether the loop iterates again, never what the loop's value is
(PAYLOAD-FREE-BREAK.md): a loop's value is its collected List, and a
value found inside a loop leaves through `return`.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC BREAK-OUTSIDE-LOOP (`break outside lexical loop`),
CONTINUE-OUTSIDE-LOOP (`continue outside lexical loop`),
RETURN-OUTSIDE-CALLABLE (`return outside callable invocation`), and
SURFACE SYNTAX for a value after `break`:

    "break" does not take a value, found integer 5

@example reject
fn first_big(xs):
    loop x in xs:
        if x > 4:
            break 5
    0

0
@caption break with a value
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 4:19
@message "break" does not take a value*
@end

@example reject
loop i from 0 to 3:
    fn stop():
        break
    stop()
@caption break in a nested function
@error CORE SEMANTIC BREAK-OUTSIDE-LOOP
@at 3:9
@end

== WHAT TO DO
To get a value out of a loop, put the loop in a function and `return` the
value; to stop collecting, `break`; to stop from inside a helper, have the
helper return a Bool and test it in the loop.

@example accept
fn first_over(xs: List[int], limit: int) -> int:
    loop x in xs:
        if x > limit:
            return x
    -1

[1, 5, 9].first_over(4)
@caption return carries the found value out of the loop
@value 5
@end

@example accept
xs = loop i from 0 to 10:
    if i == 3:
        break
    i
xs
@caption break ends a collecting loop
@value [0, 1, 2]
@end

@topic rule.imports-not-transitive
title: Imports are exact and not transitive
summary: import NS makes exactly NS available in exactly this file: not its parent or child namespaces, not what NS imports, not other files of the program.
index: rules
alias: transitive import; import a nested namespace; parent namespace import; why must I import std::str again
keywords: import, transitive, exact, nested namespace, parent, child, header, dependency
related: concept.imports concept.modules rule.method-requires-import rule.import-type rule.application-modules principle.explicit-dependencies error.missing-import error.duplicate-import
docs: IMPORTS.md

== THE RULE
`import NS` in a file's header lets that file write `NS::name` and makes
NS's functions candidates for method syntax -- for NS exactly, in this
file only:

  * not NS's parent: `import std::abi::bytes` does not import `abi`;
  * not NS's children: `import std::abi` does not import `std::abi::bytes`;
  * not what NS itself imports: `import std::test` does not give the file
    `str`, although stdlib/test.bot imports it;
  * not other files: every module and the entry program write their own.

Imports come first in a file, before any other declaration or statement,
each namespace once (DUPLICATE-IMPORT). However many files import a
namespace, it is loaded once.

== WHY
"A source file cannot refer across a namespace boundary unless it says so
in its header" (IMPORTS.md). With exact, non-transitive imports a file's
first lines are the complete list of what it depends on, and a library can
change its own imports without changing what its users can see.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
MISSING-IMPORT, at the reference -- SURFACE MODULE for a qualified name,
CORE SEMANTIC for a method call:

    namespace "std::abi" is not imported; add `import std::abi` to use `std::abi::usize`
    (imports are exact: `import std::abi::bytes` does not import "std::abi")

@example reject
import std::test

s = "ab"
std::str::length(s)
@caption Only stdlib/test.bot imports str
@error SURFACE MODULE MISSING-IMPORT
@at 4:1
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Import every namespace the file names, directly.

@example accept
import std::test
import std::str

s = "ab"
std::str::length(s)
@caption The file imports what it uses
@value 2
@end

@topic rule.module-bindings
title: What a module's top level may hold
summary: A module's top level holds only declarations, functions and immutable bindings, no statements; a binding's initializer must be context-free and its value transitively immutable.
index: rules
alias: module top level; module variable; global variable in a module; module initializer
keywords: module, top level, binding, immutable, context-free, statement, initializer, global
related: concept.modules concept.program-value rule.forward-references rule.context-installation rule.application-modules error.invalid-toplevel error.module-context error.module-immutable
docs: MODULE-BINDINGS.md IMPORTS.md CONTEXTS.md

== THE RULE
A file loaded by `import` is a module. At its top level it may hold only:

  * imports, then function definitions and declarations (`type`,
    `struct`, `enum`, `error`, `trait`, `layout`, `extern`);
  * immutable value bindings, `sizes = [4, 8, 16]`.

It may not hold expression statements (a call, a loop, an `if`), a struct
destructuring, `with context` or `return`. A binding's initializer runs
once, before the entry program, after the modules it depends on and after
the earlier bindings of its file, and:

  * must be context-free: no context, and only operations classified
    context-free (constructing a MutableVector or MutableArray is not);
  * must retain a transitively immutable value;
  * may read only earlier bindings (rule.forward-references).

Only the entry program has top-level statements; its last one is the
program's value (concept.program-value).

    # limits.bot (a module)            # main.bot (the entry program)
    sizes = [4, 8, 16]                 import limits
    fn largest() -> int:               import std::list
        16                             [limits::sizes.length(),
                                        limits::largest()]

== WHY
A module is a namespace of definitions and immutable values
(MODULE-BINDINGS.md), not a script: importing it must not run arbitrary
code or create shared state that changes. A module value is therefore a
constant every user sees alike, computed once in a fixed order, and reads
of it never need revalidation.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
In the module's file: SURFACE MODULE INVALID-TOPLEVEL for a statement
(`module "bad" (PATH): only function definitions, immutable bindings, ...
are allowed at module top level, found a "call" statement`), CORE
SEMANTIC MODULE-CONTEXT (`module binding initializer must be
context-free: native operation "std::mutable_vector::from_list" is not
classified context-free`) and MODULE-IMMUTABLE for a value that is not
proven immutable. These need a second file, so this page has no runnable
example of them.

== WHAT TO DO
Move statements into functions the entry program calls; keep mutable
state in the entry program or in values passed to functions; compute a
module value from literals and context-free functions only.

@topic rule.application-modules
title: How an import finds a module file
summary: import std::NAME is the standard library's stdlib/NAME.bot (or intrinsics); any other import NAME is the file NAME.bot beside the entry program; nothing is searched and nothing falls back.
index: rules
alias: module search path; import my own file; where does import look; standard library location
keywords: import, module, file, application root, standard library, std, stdlib, entry file, directory, reserved, rooted, ::
related: concept.modules concept.imports concept.standard-library rule.imports-not-transitive rule.module-bindings error.unknown-namespace error.reserved-namespace error.module-collision error.self-import
docs: STD-NAMESPACE.md IMPORTS.md POST-2048-PRODUCT-FIXES.md

== THE RULE
A namespace path is resolved from the root, and its first segment says
whose it is. For an `import NAME` that the entry program or one of its
own modules writes:

  * `std::NAME` is the standard library's: stdlib/NAME.bot (a nested
    `std::a::b` is stdlib/a/b.bot) beside the compiler, or a namespace of
    compiler intrinsics. Every file sees the same one;
  * any other NAME is the application module ROOT/NAME.bot (a nested `a::b`
    is ROOT/a/b.bot), ROOT the directory of the entry file;
  * nothing else is searched: not the working directory, an environment
    variable, a parent directory or a path list, and a missing `NAME` never
    falls back to `std::NAME`;
  * `std` is reserved: an application file ROOT/std/NAME.bot is never a
    module, and importing std::NAME beside one is RESERVED-NAMESPACE;
  * a leading `::` roots the path explicitly: `import ::std::list` is
    `import std::list`;
  * a standard-library module's own imports never see application files;
  * the entry file is not a module and cannot be imported (SELF-IMPORT);
    a file beside it that the program imports is a module and follows the
    module rules (rule.module-bindings).

    game/main.bot:  import score          game/score.bot:
                    import std::list        fn points(n: int) -> int:
                    score::points(4)            n * 10

An application module `list.bot` and the standard `std::list` are two
namespaces, importable side by side.

== WHY
The rule is fixed so that a name has at most one candidate file: a program
means the same thing wherever it is run from, adding a file never changes
what an import names, and the standard library cannot be shadowed
(STD-NAMESPACE.md).

== WHAT IS REPORTED
SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE names the path it looked for, and the
standard namespace of that name when there is one:

    unknown namespace "score": no such module file ROOT/score.bot (an
    application's own modules are the files beside its entry program)

SURFACE MODULE RESERVED-NAMESPACE for an application file under std/,
SELF-IMPORT for an import of the entry file.

@example reject
import score

score::points(4)
@caption No score.bot beside the entry
@error SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE
@at 1:8
@message unknown namespace "score": no such module file *
@end

@example reject
import list

0
@caption The standard list is std::list: no fallback from a missing local module
@error SURFACE MODULE UNKNOWN-NAMESPACE
@at 1:8
@message unknown namespace "list": no such module file *; the standard library's namespace of that name is std::list *
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Put the module file beside the entry file (or in a subdirectory for a
nested namespace) and run the entry file; name a standard namespace with
its std:: (`import std::list`).

@topic rule.flags
title: Flag parameters: declared last, written by name
summary: A flag is a bool parameter that is false unless its name is written at the call (f(x, :loud)); flags come last, are never computed or positional, and a function with flags is not a value.
index: rules
alias: flag parameter rules; pass a flag; computed flag; boolean option parameter
keywords: flags, flag, option, bool, :name, call, method, alias, function value
related: concept.flags principle.options-are-flags warning.many-boolean-arguments error.unknown-flag error.duplicate-flag error.flag-function-value error.argument-after-flag error.flag-not-a-value
docs: FLAGS.md

== THE RULE
  * A flag section follows the ordinary parameters: `fn label(n, flags
    :loud, :short)`. Each flag is an immutable `bool` local named without
    the colon, `true` exactly when the call writes it.
  * A call writes flags last, by name, each at most once, and only flags
    the callee declares: `label(1, :loud)`, `1.label(:loud)`.
  * A flag is never positional (`label(1, true)` is an arity error) and
    never computed: `:loud` is not a value, so it cannot be bound, passed
    or chosen at run time.
  * There are no defaults other than false, no required or negated flags.
  * A function with flags may be called (also with method syntax) or
    aliased (`g = label`), but not passed, returned or stored as a value.
  * Flags share the local namespace of the ordinary parameters.

== WHY
A flag is the language's form for an option that is "absent means false"
(FLAGS.md): its name at the call says what it does, where `f(x, true,
false)` says nothing (MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS warns on that). Flags are
part of the function's call interface, which a function value's type
(`Fn{...}`) cannot carry, so such a function stays a direct callee.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FLAG (`function label does not accept flag :quiet
(accepted flags: :loud)`), DUPLICATE-FLAG, FLAG-FUNCTION-VALUE, ARITY for
a positional Bool; SURFACE SYNTAX with the codes ARGUMENT-AFTER-FLAG,
FLAG-NOT-A-VALUE and MALFORMED-FLAG-SECTION.

@example reject
fn label(n: int, flags :loud) -> int:
    if loud:
        return n * 10
    n

label(1, :quiet)
@caption An undeclared flag
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNKNOWN-FLAG
@at 6:10
@end

== WHAT TO DO
To choose a flag at run time, choose between two calls with an `if`. An
option that is more than "on or off" is an ordinary parameter, an enum or
a struct.

@example accept
fn label(n: int, flags :loud) -> int:
    if loud:
        return n * 10
    n

fn label_for(n: int, shout: bool) -> int:
    if shout:
        return n.label(:loud)
    n.label()

[label(1), 2.label(:loud), 3.label_for(true)]
@caption Flags by name; a run-time choice
@value [1, 20, 30]
@end

@topic rule.typed-parameters
title: Declared parameter types are proven at every call
summary: A parameter annotation is a compile-time proof obligation: each call must prove its argument satisfies the declared type, and an unproven argument is rejected, never checked at run time.
index: rules
alias: typed parameter; parameter annotation; strict typed parameters; declared parameter type
keywords: parameter, type, annotation, argument, proof, domain, range, guard, contract, inferred
related: rule.type-argument rule.type-generic-call rule.type-result rule.type-field concept.functions concept.types concept.integer-domains proof.types proof.ranges rule.no-callable-types rule.struct-construction rule.method-resolution error.type
docs: STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md README.md

== THE RULE
`fn f(x: T)` makes every call of f prove that its argument's possible
values are values of T: by the argument's static type (a subtype of T),
or, for an integer domain, by what is known about its value (a literal,
or a range established by comparisons). The proof is local to the call:
it uses what is known where the call is -- guards that dominate it, the
argument's own binding and declared types, a callee's declared result --
and not what the caller's callers pass. A merely unproven argument is
rejected exactly like an incompatible one: no run-time check or
conversion is ever inserted. Inside f, x has the type T.

An untyped parameter gets a contract inferred from the function's body,
checked the same way at each call. A declared result type (`-> T`) is
proven at the function in the same way, and so is each field of a struct
construction.

== WHY
"A parameter annotation is a compile-time proof obligation, not a runtime
contract check" (STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md). The callee can rely on its
declared types without testing them, the caller learns at compile time
that a value may not fit, and no backend needs a hidden guard.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
CORE SEMANTIC TYPE, at the argument, with what the compiler knew about it:

    argument for parameter "x" cannot be proven to satisfy int[Small]
    (argument type: int, facts: [-∞, +∞])

rule.type-argument explains this message and its facts. For an untyped
parameter it adds "the parameter type inferred from the function body"
and the use it was inferred from (rule.type-generic-call); a result that
is not proven is rule.type-result, a struct field rule.type-field.

@example reject
type Small = Int in 0..10

fn twice(x: Small) -> int:
    x * 2

fn g(n: int) -> int:
    twice(n)

g(3)
@caption An int is not proven a Small
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 7:11
@message argument for parameter "x" cannot be proven*
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Establish the fact before the call: guard the argument with comparisons,
convert it through a converter function with a declared error
(rule.no-callable-types), or declare the caller's own parameter with the
narrower type, which moves the obligation to its callers.

@example accept
type Small = Int in 0..10

fn twice(x: Small) -> int:
    x * 2

fn g(n: int) -> int:
    if n >= 0 and n <= 10:
        return twice(n)
    0

g(3)
@caption A guard proves the argument is in the domain
@value 6
@end
